praiser-representation/#breadcrumb"},{"@type":"Service","name":"Appraisal Clause Representation","serviceType":"Insurance appraisal","description":"Invoking the insurance appraisal clause in Maine? Get an independent appraiser to build the scope, price it for Maine and push for a fair award.","provider":{"@id":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/#business"},"areaServed":{"@type":"State","name":"Maine"},"url":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-work/party-appraiser-representation/","image":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/assets/img/photos/consultation.webp","@id":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-work/party-appraiser-representation/#service"},{"@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I invoke appraisal if my insurer already paid part of the claim?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Usually yes. A partial payment on an accepted claim is the most common situation for appraisal, as long as the dispute is about the amount and your policy contains an appraisal clause."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does the insurance appraisal clause in Maine replace going to court?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Appraisal settles the amount of loss, and the award is generally binding on that question. Coverage, bad faith and other legal issues stay outside appraisal, so consult a Maine attorney about those."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who pays for appraisal?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Most policies have each side pay its own appraiser and split the umpire’s fee equally. Check your policy wording, since some differ."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How long do I have to demand appraisal?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Policies set their own conditions and suit-limitation periods, and many give one to two years. Read your policy and ask a Maine attorney about any deadline that worries you."}}],"@id":"https://maineinsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-work/party-appraiser-representation/#faq