<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><h2>How the appraisal clause works in a Maine policy</h2>
<p>Most homeowners, condo and commercial property policies written in Maine contain an appraisal provision. The wording varies, but the structure is familiar: if you and the insurer disagree on the <em>amount of loss</em>, either side can demand appraisal in writing. Each side then names a competent and disinterested appraiser, the two appraisers select an umpire, and an award signed by any two of the three sets the amount. Using the insurance appraisal clause in Maine is usually faster and far less expensive than litigation, and it keeps the dispute focused on dollars rather than blame.</p>
<p>The clause does not decide whether something is covered. It decides how much a covered loss is worth. That line matters, and we explain it plainly before you sign anything. Our <a href="/owner-guides/maine-appraisal-clause-plain-reading/">plain reading of the Maine appraisal clause</a> walks through typical wording sentence by sentence.</p>

<h2>What we do as your named appraiser</h2>
<p>A party appraiser is not a cheerleader. The role is to build a defensible, line-item valuation of the loss and negotiate it with the carrier’s appraiser. In practice that means:</p>
<ul>
<li>Inspecting the property, including attics, wall cavities and crawlspaces the original adjuster may have skipped.</li>
<li>Writing a full repair estimate with Maine labor rates, winter conditions and access costs built in.</li>
<li>Comparing that estimate line by line with the carrier’s, so every difference has a reason attached.</li>
<li>Negotiating with the other appraiser and, if needed, presenting the file to the umpire.</li>
<li>Reviewing the award wording so replacement cost, actual cash value and depreciation holdback are stated clearly.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Where Maine claims usually come up short</h2>
<p>The gaps we see most often are not exotic. Carriers frequently price a Portland triple-decker or a connected farmhouse in Oxford County the same as a suburban ranch. They leave out the cost of getting a crew and materials to Peaks Island or Vinalhaven. They ignore that a discontinued cedar clapboard or a red slate roof on a Hallowell Victorian cannot be patched with a near match. They assume summer production rates for work that will happen in February. Each of those is a pricing question, which is exactly what appraisal is designed to resolve.</p>
<p>Before you decide, run your numbers through the <a href="/claim-math/settlement-gap-projector/">settlement gap projector</a>. It gives a rough sense of whether the difference justifies the cost of an appraiser and a share of the umpire’s fee.</p>

<div class="note"><b class="h">When appraisal is the wrong tool</b><p>If the insurer has denied the claim outright, raised a policy exclusion, or questioned whether you complied with a condition, that is a coverage dispute. Appraisal cannot fix it. Talk to a Maine attorney first, and keep an eye on the suit-limitation period in your policy; many give one to two years, so read yours.</p></div>

<h2>Documents that strengthen the file</h2>
<p>An appraisal is only as good as its evidence. Gather the full policy with declarations, every letter and estimate from the carrier, photos and video from the day of the loss, contractor bids, invoices for emergency work such as tarping or water extraction, and any receipts for contents. Dated photos of the building before the loss help with matching and depreciation arguments. Our <a href="/claim-math/appraisal-file-strength-test/">appraisal file strength test</a> scores what you already have and flags gaps.</p>

<h2>Cost, timing and what to expect</h2>
<p>Each side pays its own appraiser, and the umpire’s fee is usually split, though your policy controls. We discuss our fee structure openly at the first call and put it in writing. Timing depends on the size of the loss and how quickly the carrier names its appraiser; straightforward residential files often resolve in weeks, while large commercial or condo losses take longer. Throughout, the Maine Bureau of Insurance remains available if you have a complaint about claim handling, and an attorney remains the right call for legal questions or deadlines.</p>
<h2>Questions about appraisal clause representation</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Can I invoke appraisal if my insurer already paid part of the claim?</summary><p>Usually yes. A partial payment on an accepted claim is the most common situation for appraisal, as long as the dispute is about the amount and your policy contains an appraisal clause.</p></details><details><summary>Does the insurance appraisal clause in Maine replace going to court?</summary><p>Appraisal settles the amount of loss, and the award is generally binding on that question. Coverage, bad faith and other legal issues stay outside appraisal, so consult a Maine attorney about those.</p></details><details><summary>Who pays for appraisal?</summary><p>Most policies have each side pay its own appraiser and split the umpire’s fee equally. Check your policy wording, since some differ.</p></details><details><summary>How long do I have to demand appraisal?</summary><p>Policies set their own conditions and suit-limitation periods, and many give one to two years. Read your policy and ask a Maine attorney about any deadline that worries you.</p></details></div>
